      "source": [
        "# Precipitation Perturbation Analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "This section implements a perturbation study to analyze how small atmospheric changes affect precipitation forecasts. We'll compare precipitation predictions from:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Baseline scenario**: Standard aurora-lite-decoder prediction without perturbations\n",
        "2. **Perturbed scenario**: Same prediction but with a small temperature perturbation applied\n",
        "\n",
        "## Methodology\n",
        "\n",
        "Based on the approach from PerturbationTries.ipynb, we apply a **Gaussian temperature perturbation** to specific atmospheric pressure levels (700-925 hPa). The perturbation is designed to represent realistic atmospheric variability:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Temperature change**: ΔT = (Lv × Δq) / cp\n",
        "  - Lv = 2.5×10⁶ J/kg (latent heat of vaporization)  \n",
        "  - cp = 1005 J/kg·K (specific heat at constant pressure)\n",
        "  - Δq = 0.001 (specific humidity perturbation)\n",
        "  - **Result**: ΔT ≈ 2.49 K maximum amplitude\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Spatial distribution**: Gaussian with σ = 8° centered at a chosen location\n",
        "- **Pressure levels**: Applied to 700, 850, and 925 hPa levels\n",
        "- **Temporal application**: Applied to both initial time steps (00:00 and 06:00)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "b441lmzrqku",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "b441lmzrqku",
        "outputId": "8383f773-128c-4d50-e6d4-bce014d6faf5"
      },
